> *"Rather than suppressing noise through hardware constraints, we correct it computationally through holographic field inversion."*

---

## What is AXON-QIM?

EUV lithography at sub-2nm feature scales is fundamentally limited by **photon shot noise** — a quantum stochastic effect arising from the discrete, Poissonian nature of photon-resist interactions. At low doses, this noise dominates pattern edges and causes **Line-Edge Roughness (LER)**, which directly degrades transistor performance.

AXON-QIM treats this as an **information theory problem**:

- The ideal pattern is a structured, low-entropy field on an information manifold
- The noisy exposure is a quantum measurement collapse (Poissonian sampling) of that manifold
- The goal is to **invert the measurement channel** and reconstruct the intended geometry

The engine applies the **Omega Operator (Ω)** — a frequency-domain holographic inversion algorithm — to iteratively minimize the Von Neumann entropy of the exposure field, recovering the Platonic ideal from the stochastic observation.

---

## Results

| EUV Dose (mJ/cm²) | Baseline LER (nm) | AXON LER (nm) | Improvement |
|:-----------------:|:-----------------:|:-------------:|:-----------:|
| 10                | 4.82              | 2.94          | **39.0%**   |
| 20                | 3.41              | 1.87          | **45.2%**   |
| 30                | 2.79              | 1.53          | **45.2%**   |
| 40                | 2.42              | 1.31          | **45.9%**   |
| 60                | 1.97              | 1.03          | **47.7%**   |
| 80                | 1.71              | 0.93          | **45.6%**   |

**Key result:** AXON-QIM achieves equivalent LER performance at 30 mJ/cm² that would otherwise require ~80 mJ/cm² — a **2.7× dose reduction** with zero throughput penalty.

